                  \\;+\;
                  (C \Gamma^{a_1 \cdots a_p})_{\alpha \beta} Z_{a_1, \cdots, a_p}
          %p
            (for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Killing+spinor"} Killing spinor
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            its corresponding
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Killing+vector"} Killing vector
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+supersymmetry+algebra"} type II supersymmetry algebra
            and the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory+supersymmetry+algebra"} M-theory supersymmetry algebra
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#AGIT89"} Azcárraga-Gauntlett-Izquierdo-Townsend 89
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity+algebra+extensions"} Lie n-algebra extensions
            of which the familiar super Lie algebra extensions are the 0-truncation (
            %a{:href => "#SatiSchreiber15"}> Sati-Schreiber 15
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Local+prequantum+field+theory"} Khavkine-Schreiber 16
          %p
            In summary, the nature and classification of Green-Schwarz action functionals captures in a mathematically precise way a good deal of the core structure of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
          %p
            In fact, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+L-infinity+algebra"} super Lie-n algebraic
            perspective on the Green-Schwarz functionals via the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/brane+bouquet"} brane bouquet
            also solves the following open problem on
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-branes"} M-branes
          %p
            it is famously known from
            = succeed "-cancellation" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freed-Witten+anomaly"} Freed-Witten anomaly
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-brane+charges"} D-brane charges
            are not in fact just in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+cohomology"} de Rham cohomology
            in every second degree, but are in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+K-theory"} twisted K-theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+homotopy+theory"} rationally
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+de+Rham+cohomology"} twisted de Rham cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F1-brane"} F1-brane
            charge (from the fundamental). It is an open problem to determine what becomes of these
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+K-theory"} twisted K-theory
            charge groups as one lifts F1/D
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            \-branes in string theory to M2/M5-branes in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th
                %th intersecting branes
                %th
                  charges in
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+cohomology+theory"} generalized cohomology theory
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F1-brane"}> F1
                  \/
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-brane"} Dp-branes
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+K-theory"} twisted K-theory
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M2-brane"}> M2
                  \/
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-branes"} M5-branes
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} ???
          %p
            Notice that there are “microscopic degrees of freedom” of the theory encoded by the choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+cohomology+theory"} generalized cohomology theory
            here, generalizing the extra degrees of freedom in the choice of a WZW-term already mentioned above. In general for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            a cohomology theory and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi E
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi ℚ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \longrightarrow E \otimes \mathbb{Q}
            its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+character"} Chern character
            map (for instance from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
            in every second degree), then a choice of genuine charges is the extra information encoded in a lift
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi E
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mtext true charge
                          %mo ↗
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mi ch
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mtext rational
                              %mrow
                                %mtext charge
                        %mtd
                          %mi E
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi ℚ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; E
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}^{\mathllap{\text{true charge}}}\nearrow &amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{ch}}
                  \\\
                  X &amp;\underset{\text{rational} \atop  \text{charge}}{\longrightarrow}&amp; E \otimes \mathbb{Q}
                  }
          %p
            But
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+homotopy+theory"} rationally
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/The+brane+bouquet"} The brane bouquet
            allows to derive this from first principles:
          %p
            Above we saw that the naive cocycles of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-branes"} D-branes
            and of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-brane"} M5-brane
            are not defined on the actual
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+super+infinity-groupoid"} smooth super infinity-groupoid
            extension of spacetime. Hence we should ask if these cocycles
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"} descend
            to the actual super-spacetime while picking up some twists.
          %p One may prove that:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the F1/D
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
                \-brane GS-WZW cocycles descend to 10d type II superspacetime to form a single cocycle in rational twisted K-theory, just as the traditional lore reqires (
                %a{:href => "#FiorenzaSatiSchreiber16"}> Fiorenza-Sati-Schreiber 16
                );
            %li
              %p
                the M2/M5 GS-WZW cocycles descent to 11d superspacetime to form a single cocycle with values in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+n-sphere"} rational 4-sphere
                (
                %a{:href => "#FiorenzaSatiSchreiber16"}> Fiorenza-Sati-Schreiber 16
                ).
          %p
            This has implications on some open conjectures regarding
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Equivariant+cohomology+of+M2%2FM5-branes"} Equivariant cohomology of M2/M5-branes
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            The Green-Schwarz action functionals are of the standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            form for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+spaces"} target spaces
            that are
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supermanifold"} super
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homogeneous+spaces"} homogeneous spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/H
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+supergroup"} Lie supergroup
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            a sub-super-group, and for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+gauge+fields"} background gauge fields
            that are super-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW+model"}> WZW
            \-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+n-bundles+with+connection"}> circle n-bundles with connection
            \/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle+gerbes"} bundle gerbes
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %ul
            %li
              for branes on
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Minkowski+spacetime"} super Minkowski spacetime
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Poincar%C3%A9+Lie+algebra"} super Poincaré Lie algebra
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔥
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{h}
              the
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentz+Lie+algebra"} Lorentz Lie algebra
          %p
            These action functionals were first considered in (
            %a{:href => "#GreenSchwarz84"}> Green-Schwarz 84
            ) for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstrings"} superstrings
            in various dimensions. The full interpretation of the action functional as an higher
            = succeed "-type" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wess-Zumino-Witten+theory"} Wess-Zumino-Witten theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra+cohomology"} Lie algebra cohomology
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Poincar%C3%A9+Lie+algebra"} super Poincaré Lie algebra
            (or rather of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+translation+Lie+algebra"} super translation Lie algebra
            inside it) is due to (
            %a{:href => "#AzcarragaTownsend89"}> Azcárraga-Townsend89
            ).
          %ul
            %li
              for branes on
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+anti+de+Sitter+spacetime"} super anti de Sitter spacetime
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superconformal+group"} superconformal group
              (e.g.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "#MetsaevTseytlin98"} Metsaev-Tseytlin 98, section 3
          %h3#Supercoordinates Supercoordinates
          %p
            We briefly review some basics of the canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinates"} coordinates
            and the super
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra+cohomology"} Lie algebra cohomology
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Poincar%C3%A9+Lie+algebra"} super Poincaré Lie algebra
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Minkowski+space"} super Minkowski space
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#AzcarragaTownsend89"} Azcárraga-Townsend 89
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Cartesian+space"} super Cartesian space
            and at
            = succeed ".)." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/signs+in+supergeometry"} signs in supergeometry
          %p
            By the general discussion at
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chevalley-Eilenberg+algebra"} Chevalley-Eilenberg algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Poincar%C3%A9+Lie+algebra"} super Poincaré Lie algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔰𝔦𝔰𝔬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{siso}(D-1,1)
            by its CE-algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔰𝔦𝔰𝔬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CE(\mathfrak{siso}(D-1,1))
            “of
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left-invariant+1-forms"} left-invariant 1-forms
          #CEAlgebraOfSuperPoincare.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chevalley-Eilenberg+algebra"} Chevalley-Eilenberg algebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi CE
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝔰𝔦𝔰𝔬
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi d
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CE(\mathfrak{siso}(d-1,1))
              is generated on
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  elements
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %msup
                          %mi e
                          %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{e^a\}
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %msup
                          %mi ω
                          %mrow
                            %mi a
                            %mi b
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\omega^{ a b}\}
                  of degree
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mi even
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1,even)
              %li
                %p
                  and elements
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %msup
                          %mi ψ
                          %mi α
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\psi^\alpha\}
                  of degree
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mi odd
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1,odd)
            %p with the differential defined by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi CE
                    %msup
                      %mi ω
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mi b
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi ω
                      %mi a
                    %msub
                      %mrow
                      %mi b
                    %mo ∧
                    %msup
                      %mi ω
                      %mrow
                        %mi b
                        %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    d_{CE} \omega^{a b} = \omega^a{}_b \wedge \omega^{b c}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi CE
                    %msup
                      %mi e
                      %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi ω
                      %mi a
                    %msub
                      %mrow
                      %mi b
                    %mo ∧
                    %msup
                      %mi e
                      %mi b
                    %mo +
                    %mfrac
                      %mi i
                      %mn 2
                    %mover
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %msup
                      %mi Γ
                      %mi a
                    %mi ψ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    d_{CE} e^{a } = \omega^a{}_b \wedge e^b + \frac{i}{2}\bar \psi \Gamma^a \psi
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi CE
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo =
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 4
                    %msup
                      %mi ω
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mi b
                    %msub
                      %mi Γ
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mi b
                    %mi ψ
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    d_{CE} \psi = \frac{1}{4} \omega^{ a b} \Gamma_{a b} \psi
                    \\,.
            %p
              Removing the terms involving
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
              here this is the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+translation+algebra"} super translation algebra
          %p
            In this way the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super-Poincar%C3%A9+Lie+algebra"} super-Poincaré Lie algebra
            and its extensions is usefully discussed for instance in (
            %a{:href => "#DAuriaFre82"}> D’Auria-Fré 82
            ) and in (
            %a{:href => "#AzcarragaTownsend89"}> Azcárraga-Townsend 89
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#CAIB99"} CAIB 99
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinates"} coordinates
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Minkowski+space"} super Minkowski space
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              The abstract generators in def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CEAlgebraOfSuperPoincare"}
              are identified with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+invariant+1-forms"} left invariant 1-forms
              on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super-translation+group"} super-translation group
              (=
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Minkowski+space"} super Minkowski space
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi θ
                      %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x^a, \theta^\alpha)
              be the canonical
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinates"} coordinates
              on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supermanifold"} supermanifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{d|N}
              underlying the super translation group. Then the identification is
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi ψ
                          %mi α
                        %mo =
                        %mi d
                        %msup
                          %mi θ
                          %mi α
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi^\alpha = d \theta^\alpha
                  \.
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi e
                          %mi a
                        %mo =
                        %mi d
                        %msup
                          %mi x
                          %mi a
                        %mo +
                        %mfrac
                          %mi i
                          %mn 2
                        %mover
                          %mi θ
                          %mo ¯
                        %msup
                          %mi Γ
                          %mi a
                        %mi d
                        %mi θ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e^a = d x^a + \frac{i}{2} \overline{\theta} \Gamma^a d \theta
                  \.
            %p
              Notice that this then gives the above formula for the differential of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super-vielbein"} super-vielbein
              in def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CEAlgebraOfSuperPoincare"}
              as
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi d
                            %msup
                              %mi e
                              %mi a
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mi d
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi d
                            %msup
                              %mi x
                              %mi a
                            %mo +
                            %mfrac
                              %mi i
                              %mn 2
                            %mover
                              %mi θ
                              %mo ¯
                            %msup
                              %mi Γ
                              %mi a
                            %mi d
                            %mi θ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mfrac
                              %mi i
                              %mn 2
                            %mi d
                            %mover
                              %mi θ
                              %mo ¯
                            %msup
                              %mi Γ
                              %mi a
                            %mi d
                            %mi θ
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mfrac
                              %mi i
                              %mn 2
                            %mover
                              %mi ψ
                              %mo ¯
                            %msup
                              %mi Γ
                              %mi a
                            %mi ψ
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\begin{aligned}
                    d e^a &amp; = d (d x^a + \frac{i}{2} \overline{\theta} \Gamma^a d \theta)
                    \\\
                    \&amp; = \frac{i}{2} d \overline{\theta}\Gamma^a d \theta
                    \\\
                    \&amp; = \frac{i}{2} \overline{\psi}\Gamma^a \psi
                    \\end{aligned}
                    \\,.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              The term
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mfrac
                      %mi i
                      %mn 2
                    %mover
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %msup
                      %mi Γ
                      %mi a
                    %mi ψ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{i}{2}\bar \psi \Gamma^a \psi
              is sometimes called the
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supertorsion"} supertorsion
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super-vielbein"} super-vielbein
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi e
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi CE
                    %msup
                      %mi e
                      %mi a
                    %mo −
                    %msup
                      %mi ω
                      %mi a
                    %msub
                      %mrow
                      %mi b
                    %mo ∧
                    %msup
                      %mi e
                      %mi b
                    %mo =
                    %mfrac
                      %mi i
                      %mn 2
                    %mover
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %msup
                      %mi Γ
                      %mi a
                    %mi ψ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    d_{CE} e^{a } -\omega^a{}_b \wedge e^b = \frac{i}{2}\bar \psi \Gamma^a \psi
            %p
              may be read as saying that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
              is
              = succeed "-free" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsion"} torsion
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi CE
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝔰𝔦𝔰𝔬
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CE(\mathfrak{siso})
              which have “all indices contracted”. (See also at
              = succeed ".)" do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsion+constraints+in+supergravity"} torsion constraints in supergravity
            %p Notably we have
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mover
                        %mi ψ
                        %mo ¯
                      %mo ∧
                      %msup
                        %mi Γ
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi a
                            %mn 1
                          %mi ⋯
                          %msub
                            %mi a
                            %mi p
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo ∧
                      %msub
                        %mi e
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi a
                            %mn 1
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo ∧
                      %msub
                        %mi e
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi a
                            %mi p
                      %mo )
                    %mo ∝
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mover
                        %mi ψ
                        %mo ¯
                      %mo ∧
                      %msup
                        %mi Γ
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi a
                            %mn 1
                          %mi ⋯
                          %msub
                            %mi a
                            %mi p
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo ∧
                      %msub
                        %mi e
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi a
                            %mn 1
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo ∧
                      %msub
                        %mi e
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi a
                            %mrow
                              %mi p
                              %mo −
                              %mn 1
                      %mo )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mover
                        %mi Ψ
                        %mo ¯
                      %mo ∧
                      %msub
                        %mi Γ
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi a
                            %mi p
                      %mi Ψ
                      %mo )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    d \left(
                    \\overline{\psi} \wedge \Gamma^{a_1 \cdots a_p} \psi
                    \\wedge e_{a_1} \wedge \cdots \wedge e_{a_p}
                    \\right)
                    \\propto
                    \\left(
                    \\overline{\psi} \wedge \Gamma^{a_1 \cdots a_p} \psi
                    \\wedge e_{a_1} \wedge \cdots \wedge e_{a_{p-1}}
                    \\right)
                    \\wedge
                    \\left(
                    \\overline{\Psi} \wedge \Gamma_{a_p} \Psi
                    \\right)
                    \\,.
            %p
              This remaining operation “
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo ↦
                    %msup
                      %mi Ψ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e \mapsto \Psi^2
              ” of the differential acting on Loretz scalars is sometimes denoted “
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_0
              ”, e.g. in (
              %a{:href => "#BossardHoweStelle09"}> Bossard-Howe-Stelle 09, equation (8)
              ).
            %p
              This relation is what govers all of the exceptional super Lie algebra cocycles that appear as WZW terms for the Green-Schwarz action below: for some combinations of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi D
                    %mo ,
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (D,p)
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fierz+identity"} Fierz identity
              implies that the term
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mover
                        %mi ψ
                        %mo ¯
                      %mo ∧
                      %msup
                        %mi Γ
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi a
                            %mn 1
                          %mi ⋯
                          %msub
                            %mi a
                            %mi p
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo ∧
                      %msub
                        %mi e
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi a
                            %mn 1
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo ∧
                      %msub
                        %mi e
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi a
                            %mrow
                              %mi p
                              %mo −
                              %mn 1
                      %mo )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mover
                        %mi Ψ
                        %mo ¯
                      %mo ∧
                      %msub
                        %mi Γ
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi a
                            %mi p
                      %mi Ψ
                      %mo )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\left(
                    \\overline{\psi} \wedge \Gamma^{a_1 \cdots a_p} \psi
                    \\wedge e_{a_1} \wedge \cdots \wedge e_{a_{p-1}}
                    \\right)
                    \\wedge
                    \\left(
                    \\overline{\Psi} \wedge \Gamma_{a_p} \Psi
                    \\right)
            %p vanishes identically, and hence in these dimensions the term
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo ∧
                    %msup
                      %mi Γ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mn 1
                        %mi ⋯
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mi p
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo ∧
                    %msub
                      %mi e
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mn 1
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ∧
                    %msub
                      %mi e
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\overline{\psi} \wedge \Gamma^{a_1 \cdots a_p} \psi
                    \\wedge e_{a_1} \wedge \cdots \wedge e_{a_p}
            %p
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycle"} cocycle
              %a{:href => "#BraneScan"} brane scan
              table below.
          %h3#kinetic_term Kinetic term
          %p (…)
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kinetic+action"} kinetic action
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msup
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo *
                  %msup
                    %mi Π
                    %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo *
                  %msup
                    %mi Π
                    %mi b
                  %msub
                    %mi η
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\int_\Sigma \langle \phi^\ast\Pi^a, \phi^\ast \Pi^b \eta_{a b}\rangle
          %p (…)
          %h3#DefinitionWZWTerm WZW term
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi ω
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi ψ
                    %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (e^a, \omega^{a b}, \psi^\alpha)
            be the standard generators of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chevalley-Eilenberg+algebra"} Chevalley-Eilenberg algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔰𝔦𝔰𝔬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CE(\mathfrak{siso}(d,1))
            of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Poincar%C3%A9+Lie+algebra"} super Poincaré Lie algebra
          %p
            The part of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra+cohomology"} Lie algebra cohomology
            of the super translation Lie algebra that is invariant under the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+orthogonal+Lie+algebra"} Lorentz
            transformations is spanned by closed elements of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mover
                    %mi θ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %msub
                    %mi Γ
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mi p
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi d
                  %mi θ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %msup
                    %mi Π
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 1
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ∧
                  %msup
                    %mi Π
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mi p
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mu =
                  (d \bar \theta \Gamma_{a_1, \cdots, a_p} \wedge d \theta)
                  \\wedge
                  \\Pi^{a_1} \wedge \cdots \wedge \Pi^{a_p}
                  \\,.
          %p
            These exist (are closed) only for certain combinations of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#BraneScan"} below
          %p
            For a bosonic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW+model"} WZW model
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+gauge+field"} background gauge field
            induced by such a cocycle would be the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            to a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+n-bundle+with+connection"} circle n-bundle with connection
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+lemma"} Poincaré lemma
            applies and these circle
            = succeed "-connections" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+an+infinity-bundle"} connection
            form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi β
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
            satisfying
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mi β
                  %mo =
                  %mi μ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d \beta = \mu
                  \\,.
          %p The WZW part of the GS action is then
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi WZW
                  %mo :
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi Σ
                  %msup
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo *
                  %mi β
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S_{WZW } : \phi \mapsto \int_\Sigma \phi^* \beta
          %p (…)
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#KappaSymmetry
            Siegel- or
            = succeed "-symmetry" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
          %p
            The Green-Schwarz action has an extra fermionic symmetry, on top of the genuine supersymmetry, first observed in (
            %a{:href => "#Siegel83"}> Siegel 83
            ) for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superparticle"} superparticle
            and in (
            %a{:href => "#Siegel84"}> Siegel 84
            ) for the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+1-brane+in+3d"} super 1-brane in 3d
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#GreenSchwarz"} GreenSchwarz 84
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kappa-symmetry"} kappa-symmetry
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} super
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+geometry"} Cartan geometry
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
            (
            %a{:href => "#McArthur"}> McArthur
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#GKW"} GKW
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%27Auria-Fr%C3%A9+formulation+of+supergravity"} D'Auria-Fré formulation of supergravity
            is in (
            %a{:href => "#AFFFTT98"}> AFFFTT 98, section 3
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#FreGrassi07"} Fré-Grassi 07, section 2.2
          %h3#BraneScan Dimensions – the brane scan
          %p
            The Green-Schwarz action functional of a
            = succeed "-brane" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p,d)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra+cohomology"} super Lie algebra cocycles
            on the super translation Lie algebra (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#DefinitionWZWTerm"} above
          %p
            The corresponding table has been called the
            %strong brane scan
            in the literature, now often called the “old brane scan”, since it has meanwhile been further completed (see below). In (
            %a{:href => "#Duff87"}> Duff 87
            ) the “old brane scan” is displayed as follows.
          %p
            %img{:height => "500", :src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/DuffBraneScan.jpg"}/
          %p
            In the
            = succeed "-row" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo =
                    %mn 10
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D = 10
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"} superstring
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            and its
            = succeed "," do
