            %h3#the_brane_molecule The Brane molecule
            %p
              Furthermore, there exists a more general classification of possible supermembranes in spacetime with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              spatial dimensions and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              time dimensions, appearing in (
              %a{:href => "#BlencoweDuff88"}> Blencowe-Duff 88
              ). In this sense, the brane scan is but the
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T=1
              branch of the brane molecule. The objects appearing here are expected to be related to other generalizations of string theory. See
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%3D12+supergravity"} D=12 supergravity
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bosonic+M-theory"} bosonic M-theory
            %p
              %img{:alt => "The brane molecule without assuming super Poincare invariance.", :src => "/nlab/files/branemoleculepc.png"}/
            %p Compare:
            %ul
              %li#BlencoweDuff88
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Miles+Blencowe"}> Miles Blencowe
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Duff"} Mike Duff
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Supermembranes and the Signature of Space-time
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "inspirehep.net/record/262142"} spire:262142
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0550-3213(88)90155-1"} 10.1016/0550-3213(88)90155-1
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a{:href => "http://inspirehep.net/record/262142/files/cer-000099708.pdf"} pdf
              %li
                %p
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane+bouquet"} brane bouquet
          %h3#OnCurvedSpacetime On curved spacetime and supergravity equations of motion
          %p
            In the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+order+formulation+of+gravity"} first order formulation of gravity
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
            configuration on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+connection"} Cartan connection
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi SuperPoincare
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi conn
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\nabla \colon X \to \mathbf{B} SuperPoincare(d-1,1)_{conn}
          %p
            hence a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+connection"} principal connection
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Poincar%C3%A9+group"} super Poincaré group
            such such that at each point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
            it identifies the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+space"} tangent space
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo ;
                      %mi N
                  %mo =
                  %mi 𝔰𝔦𝔰𝔬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi 𝔬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{d;N} = \mathfrak{siso}(d-1,1)/\mathfrak{o}(d-1,1)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi x
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mo ∇
                  %mi 𝔰𝔦𝔰𝔬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo ;
                      %mi N
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  T_x X \stackrel{\nabla}{\longrightarrow}
                  \\mathfrak{siso}(d-1,1)
                  \\longrightarrow
                  \\mathbb{R}^{d;N}
                  \\,.
          %p
            Hence given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra+cocycle"} Lie algebra cocycle
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathfrak{g} \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}[2]
          %p
            as for the Green-Schwarz superstring we can
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback+of+differential+forms"} pull it back
            along this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+connection"} Cartan connection
            to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+3-form"} differential 3-form
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
          %p
            In general this 3-form is no longer
            = succeed "." do
              %em closed
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\nabla)
            as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW+model"} WZW model
          %p
            The claim now is that requiring this 3-form still to be closed is, as a condition on the field of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∇
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            (the super-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+equations"}> Einstein equations
            ).
          %p
            This is due to (
            %a{:href => "#Nilsson81"}> Nilsson 81
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#BergshoeffSezginTownsend86"} Bergshoeff-Sezgin-Townsend 86
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#ReferencesSupergravityBackgroundEquationsOfMotion"} below
          %h4#MembraneIn11dSuGraBackground Membrane in 11d SuGra background
          %p
            For the
            = succeed "(" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/membrane"} membrane
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M2-brane"} M2-brane
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
            (
            %a{:href => "#BergshoeffSezginTownsend87"}> Bergshoeff-Sezgin-Townsend 87
            ) find that consistency requires that (in a given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate+chart"} coordinate chart
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super-vielbein+field"} super-vielbein field
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi E
                      %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi E
                      %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi Ψ
                      %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E^A) = (E^a, \Psi^\alpha)
          #eq:HConstraintEquationForMembraneIn11d.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi H
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mover
                            %mi Ψ
                            %mo ¯
                          %mo ∧
                          %msup
                            %mi Γ
                            %mi ab
                          %mi Ψ
                          %mo ∧
                          %msub
                            %mi E
                            %mi a
                          %mo ∧
                          %msub
                            %mi E
                            %mi b
                          %mo +
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi d
                          %msub
                            %mi C
                            %mn 3
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %msub
                            %mi Γ
                            %mrow
                              %mi a
                              %mi b
                              %mi α
                              %mi β
                          %msup
                            %mi E
                            %mi a
                          %mo ∧
                          %msup
                            %mi E
                            %mi b
                          %mo ∧
                          %msup
                            %mi E
                            %mi α
                          %mo ∧
                          %msup
                            %mi E
                            %mi β
                          %mo +
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi d
                          %msub
                            %mi C
                            %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  H &amp; = \overline{\Psi}\wedge\Gamma^{ab} \Psi \wedge E_a \wedge E_b + \mathbf{d}C_3
                  \\\
                  \&amp; =
                  \\Gamma_{a b \alpha \beta} E^a \wedge E^b \wedge E^\alpha \wedge E^\beta +
                  \\mathbf{d}C_3
                  \\end{aligned}
          %p
            where the first summand is the super-Lie algebra cocycle that classifies the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+Lie+3-algebra"} supergravity Lie 3-algebra
            and the second is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} supergravity C-field
            proper (hence a purely bosonic differential form). In the second line we have rewritten this more manifestly in terms of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super-vielbein"} super-vielbein
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi E
                      %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi E
                      %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi E
                      %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi E
                      %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi Ψ
                      %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E^A) = (E^a, E^\alpha) = (E^a, \Psi^\alpha)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%E2%82%82-manifolds"} G₂-manifolds
            (see
            %a{:href => "G2+manifold#DefiniteFormsInTermsOfVielbeinFields"} this example
            for details).
          %p
            Moreover the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsion"} torsion
            tensor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is to have its
            = succeed "-component" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mi a
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi α
                    %msub
                      %mrow
                      %mi β
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (T^a)^\alpha{}_\beta
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi Γ
                      %mi a
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi α
                    %msub
                      %mrow
                      %mi β
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Gamma^a)^\alpha{}_\beta
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsion+constraints+in+supergravity"} torsion constraints in supergravity
          %p
            In addition the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bianchi+identities"} Bianchi identities
            have to hold:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∇
                      %msup
                        %mi T
                        %mi A
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi E
                        %mi B
                      %mo ∧
                      %msub
                        %mi R
                        %mi B
                      %msup
                        %mrow
                        %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla T^A = E^B \wedge R_{B}{}^{A}
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∇
                      %mi H
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla H = 0
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+derivative"}> covariant constancy
                ).
          %p
            All this is implied by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
          %p
            Notice that in view of the above analogy to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%E2%82%82-structure"} G₂-structure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%E2%82%82-manifold"} G₂-manifold
            structure.
          %p
            Discussion of this in the somewhat more streamlined
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%27Auria-Fr%C3%A9+formulation+of+supergravity"} D'Auria-Fré formulation of supergravity
            is in (
            %a{:href => "#AFFFTT98"}> AFFFTT 98, section 3.1
            ).
          %h4#heterotic_string Heterotic string
          %p
            Discussion that for the GS-version of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string"} heterotic string
            consistency of the background is equivalent to the equations of motion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+supergravity"} heterotic supergravity
            is in (
            %a{:href => "#ShapiroTaylor87"}> Shapiro-Taylor 87
            ).
          %p
            Discussion with the hetetoric gauge field included is in (
            %a{:href => "#AtickDharRatra86"}> Atick-Dhar-Ratra 86
            ).
          %h4#type_ii_string Type II string
          %p
            Discussion for the GS-version of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+superstring"} type II superstring
            in
            = succeed "-backgrounds" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+supergravity"} type II supergravity
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#GHMNT85"} GHMNT 85
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-branes"} D-branes
            in type II in (
            %a{:href => "#CGNSW97"}> CGNSW 97
            ).
          %h3#ConservedCurrents Conserved currents
          %p
            The super-WZW term of the GS action functionals is invariant under
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            only up to a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/divergence"} divergence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Noether+theorem"} Noether theorem
            in its generality for “weak” symmetries applies and gives that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conserved+currents"} conserved currents
            receive an extra contribution from this divergence term. The resulting algebra is a central extension of the given
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+translation+Lie+algebra"} super translation Lie algebra
            %a{:href => "super+Poincare+Lie+algebra#PolyvectorExtensions"} polyvector extensions
            “by brane charges” of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Poincar%C3%A9+Lie+algebra"} super Poincaré Lie algebra
            (
            %a{:href => "#AGIT89"}> AGIT 89
            ).
          %h3#AsPartOfTheAdSCFTCorrespodence As part of the AdS-CFT correspondence
          %p
            By the above discussion, Green-Schwarz super
            = succeed "-branes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superspacetimes"} superspacetimes
            that satisfy the respective higher
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+branes"} black branes
            in essentially just the combinations of dimensions and supersymmetries that the original Green-Schwarz sigma-models exist in, hence they look precisely like the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative
            avatars of whatever these
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma+models"} sigma models
            give the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
            of by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second+quantization"} second quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+holes+in+string+theory"} black holes in string theory
            for more on this correspondence between branes in string perturbation theory and black branes in supergravity.)
          %p
            Moreover, the near-horizon geometries of these
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+branes"} black branes
            are always
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti+de+Sitter+spacetime"} anti de Sitter spacetime
            times orthogonal directions.
          %p Therefore it is natural to consider the perturbation of the Green-Schwarz sigma-models around their asymptotic embeddings into AdS spaces, hence effectively the perturbation theory of the degrees of freedom at those naked singularity at which the corresponding black brane sits.
          %p
            After
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphism"} diffeomorphism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fixing"} gauge fixing
            one finds that the resulting field theories now on the
            = succeed "-brane" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolumes"} worldvolumes
            are precisely the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superconformal+field+theories"} superconformal field theories
            for all the
            %a{:href => "supersymmetry#ClassificationSuperconformalInDimgt2"} allowed superconformal supersymmetries
            (see also at
            = succeed "):" do
