          %h2#references References
          %h3#SuperStringAsAGSSigmaModel Super-string as a GS-sigma model
          %p A precursor to the actual Green-Schwarz action functional is
          %ul
            %li#GreenSchwarz81
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Green"}> Michael Green
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Schwarz"} John Schwarz
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Supersymmetrical Dual String Theory
            %li#GreenSchwarz82
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Green"}> Michael Green
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Schwarz"} John Schwarz
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Supersymmetrical Dual String Theory. 2. Vertices and Trees
          %p
            which presented a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/light-cone+gauge+quantization"} light-cone gauge quantization
            of superstring with manifest
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
          %p
            The observation that this has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+covariance"} generally covariant
            formulation lead to what is now called the Green-Schwarz action functional proper, for the superstring:
          %ul
            %li#GreenSchwarz84
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Green"}> Michael Green
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Schwarz"} John Schwarz
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Covariant description of superstrings
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://inspirehep.net/record/193596"} spire:193596
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0370-2693(84)92021-5"} arXiv;10.1016/0370-2693(84)92021-5
            %li#GreenSchwarz84b
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Green"}> Michael Green
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Schwarz"} John Schwarz
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Properties of the Covariant Formulation of Superstring Theories
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://inspirehep.net/record/196623"} spire:196623
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0550-3213(84)90030-0"} doi:10.1016/0550-3213(84)90030-0
          %p See also the historical comments in
          %ul
            %li#Schwarz16
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Schwarz"}> John Schwarz
              , slides 24-25 of
              = succeed "," do
                %em String Theory in the Twentieth Century
              %a{:href => "http://ymsc.tsinghua.edu.cn:8090/strings/"} Strings 2016
              (
              %a{:href => "http://ymsc.tsinghua.edu.cn:8090/strings/slides/8.1/Schwarz.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            Early discussion of coupling the GS superstring to general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+fields"} background fields
            in
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo =
                    %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D=4
          %ul
            %li#Gates90
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S.+James+Gates+Jr."}> S. James Gates Jr.
              \:
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  Progress Toward Covariant Formulation of All
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                        %mo =
                        %mn 4
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D=4
                  GS-type
                  = succeed "-model" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi σ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
              = succeed "," do
                %em Superstrings and Particle Theory
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/1051"} doi:10.1142/1051
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Gates-D4GSModels.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p A standard textbook reference for the GS superstring is
          %ul
            %li#GreenSchwarzWitten
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Green"}> Michael Green
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Schwarz"} John Schwarz
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"} Edward Witten
              = succeed "," do
                %em Superstring theory
          %p and a brief paragraph in Volume II, section 10.2, page 983 of
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eric+D%27Hoker"}> Eric D'Hoker
                ,
                %em String theory – lecture 10: Supersymmetry and supergravity
                , in part 3 of
              %p
